WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.950
Before we touch any software, let's understand the business. Why does this job exist at all?

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:10.176
It all comes down to two companies that want to trade with each other.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:15.951
Meet Glacier Coolers. They make coolers. They are the vendor, sometimes called the supplier.

00:00:15.951 --> 00:00:20.926
And meet BigBox Retail, a big store chain. They are the retailer, the buyer.

00:00:20.926 --> 00:00:25.551
BigBox wants Glacier's coolers on its shelves, so shoppers can buy them.

00:00:25.551 --> 00:00:31.826
When two companies exchange business documents like this, each one calls the other a trading partner.

00:00:31.826 --> 00:00:40.101
At LYSI, our client is usually the vendor, like Glacier. And the trading partner is usually the retailer they sell to.

00:00:40.101 --> 00:00:44.776
Now, every sale follows the same simple cycle. Think of a restaurant.

00:00:44.776 --> 00:00:50.401
First, the retailer places an order. That's the customer telling the waiter what they want.

00:00:50.401 --> 00:00:56.026
Second, the vendor ships the goods, and tells the retailer exactly what is in each box.

00:00:56.026 --> 00:00:59.126
Third, the vendor sends a bill, the invoice.

00:00:59.126 --> 00:01:04.276
And fourth, the retailer pays. Order, ship, bill, pay. Then it starts again.

00:01:04.276 --> 00:01:10.226
Here's the problem. A big retailer does this with thousands of vendors, every single day.

00:01:10.226 --> 00:01:15.351
Doing it by email, phone, or paper would be slow, and full of typing mistakes.

00:01:15.351 --> 00:01:23.526
So instead, the two computers talk directly, using a strict shared format. That's EDI, Electronic Data Interchange.

00:01:23.526 --> 00:01:35.376
Each step becomes a standard document with a number. The order is an eight fifty. The ship notice is an eight fifty six. The invoice is an eight ten. And the payment is an eight twenty.

00:01:35.376 --> 00:01:39.626
You'll hear those numbers every day, so it's worth remembering them now.

00:01:39.626 --> 00:01:47.826
But retailers are strict. If a ship notice is late, or wrong, they charge the vendor a fine. That fine is called a chargeback.

00:01:47.826 --> 00:01:54.901
A few small mistakes, repeated across many orders, can cost a vendor real money. So accuracy matters.

00:01:54.901 --> 00:02:04.826
That's where we come in. Glacier's system and BigBox's system sit on each side, and Orderful sits in the middle, translating and checking every document.

00:02:04.826 --> 00:02:12.076
Our job is to connect them, test them, and keep the documents flowing correctly, so our client gets paid on time.

00:02:12.076 --> 00:02:18.376
Order, ship, bill, pay. Keep that cycle in mind, and everything else in this course will make sense.
